<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyala River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sirwan River, Sirwan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diyala is a river rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where it is called the Sirwan, and flowing through north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Its valley was a main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> and was home to ancient cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>. Its lower course has been irrigated since early antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diyala-river.jpg" alt="Diyala River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ali Al Obaidi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diyala_River_in_Baghdad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
